  <div class="fc-delta-reason">DA ruling accepted. Conditional probability structure: P(F13=YES) × P(>=25/day | ceasefire within 65d) + P(F13=NO) × P(>=25/day | no ceasefire) = 0.45 × 0.28 + 0.55 × 0.03 = 0.143, rounded to 0.15. IMF PortWatch July 13-19 average approximately 10 vessels/day against pre-crisis baseline of approximately 88/day. Lloyd's List July 21: 70% AIS-dark, inbound movements 6 per week. The 10-15 vessel gap to 25/day requires formal cessation plus several weeks of insurance-market and navigator-confidence normalization. Probability reduced from 0.25 to 0.15.</div>
  <dl class="fc-meta-row">
    <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>YES if IMF PortWatch records a 7-consecutive-day rolling average of at least 25 commercial vessel transits per day through the Strait of Hormuz, confirmed by at least one OSINT-grade secondary source, before EOD September 30, 2026.</dd>
    <dt>Sources</dt><dd>Primary: IMF PortWatch (portwatch.imf.org). Secondary: Lloyd's List Intelligence Hormuz Brief, UKMTO situation reports, NBC News Hormuz tracker.</dd>
    <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>AIS-dark transits not independently verified by a second OSINT source = excluded from the count. Military vessel transits = excluded. Average must be computed over a rolling 7-day window, not a single-day spike. PortWatch methodology change mid-horizon = flag for human review before resolution.</dd>
  </dl>
