  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      
<p>For the third consecutive night, CENTCOM issued no public announcement of offensive airstrikes against Iranian territory. The last confirmed strike release was titled &#8220;U.S. Concludes 13th Night of Strikes on Iranian Military Targets,&#8221; published approximately July 23. On July 26, US Ambassador to the United Nations Mike Waltz told reporters that President Trump was &#8220;giving some talks some space&#8221; and that negotiations were proceeding &#8220;at every level, from the more technical aspect of it all the way to the highest levels.&#8221; Trump maintained concurrent rhetoric about the US being &#8220;locked and loaded&#8221; but confirmed no strikes had taken place. CENTCOM&#8217;s public releases page showed no new Iran strike announcement in the 48-hour window ending July 27. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

<p>Iranian armed forces spokesperson Amir Mohammad Akraminia issued a public statement Sunday confirming Tehran had &#8220;suspended operations&#8221; against US-affiliated targets in the region as a &#8220;direct response&#8221; to Washington refraining from attacks for two consecutive nights. Akraminia characterized Iran&#8217;s posture as &#8220;based on reciprocity&#8221; and warned explicitly that the conflict would &#8220;expand&#8221; if US strikes resumed. Iran International and CBS News both confirmed no new Iranian interdiction attempts against commercial vessels in the Strait of Hormuz were reported in the 48-hour window. The Iranian Army statement did not use the term &#8220;ceasefire&#8221; and specified no duration. → supports <span class="cite">F13</span> without move</p>

<p>Qatar and Pakistan jointly submitted a 10-day ceasefire framework to both Washington and Tehran on approximately July 24&#8211;26, calling for both sides to return to pre-July 7 positions as a precondition for resuming the June 17 MOU process. Omani-led talks on Hormuz navigation rights &#8220;made progress,&#8221; per two regional sources cited by CBS News. Iran&#8217;s foreign ministry did not formally reject the Qatar/Pakistan framework in the 48-hour window &#8212; a departure from its July 24 rejection of the Iraqi-mediated US proposal and its July 20 rejection of three separate US-backed proposals. A JPost exclusive from approximately July 25 reported that Iran had also secretly submitted its own 10-day ceasefire proposal to mediators during the strike campaign, establishing that Tehran held a positive-sum exit formulation concurrent with the public rejections. No joint statement from both capitals emerged. → moved <span class="cite">F13</span>, <span class="cite">F14</span></p>

<p>No substantive activity on the three pillars of a comprehensive final agreement &#8212; nuclear program and IAEA access, Hormuz freedom of navigation terms, and the US sanctions framework &#8212; was documented in the last 48 hours. IAEA Director-General Rafael Grossi issued no statement on monitoring access to Fordow, Natanz, or Esfahan during this period. US special envoy Steve Witkoff and Iranian chief negotiator Abbas Araghchi were not reported in proximity or confirmed contact during the July 26&#8211;27 window. The 60-day MOU clock, which began June 17, carries approximately 20 days of remaining negotiating runway before expiry. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

<p>Three July 2026 Israeli polls published between July 17 and July 22 placed the Netanyahu-led coalition bloc at 48&#8211;51 seats &#8212; a deficit of 10&#8211;13 seats versus the 61-seat threshold. A JPost poll gave the coalition 48 seats and the opposition 62. A July 21 Channel 12 poll had Gadi Eisenkot&#8217;s Yashar party at 24 seats, placing it above Likud at 23 for the first time in this cycle. Haaretz characterized the July 17 data as the bloc&#8217;s &#8220;lowest point yet.&#8221; No UTJ or Shas reconciliation with Netanyahu&#8217;s Likud electoral framework was reported during the July 26&#8211;27 period. The October 27 election date remained unchanged. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

